if(A.dE(g,A.a(["pcos","weight gain","weight"],k)))return"PCOS can make weight easier to gain for some people, often through insulin resistance, androgens, sleep, and appetite. It does not happen to everyone with PCOS, and weight gain has many other causes. Reading about it does not mean you have PCOS.\n\nWhat helps a clinical visit is a plain record: cycle spacing, skin, hair growth, and how you feel after meals. Strength training, protein at meals, and a steady bedtime are reasonable supports. They are not a treatment plan.\n\nA clinician can look at androgens and metabolic labs if that fits your story. This reply will not start you on a medication or a crash diet."
if(A.dE(g,A.a(["ferritin","iron"],k)))return"Ferritin is a storage form of iron. Heavy periods can draw it down, and low stores can show up as fatigue or hair shedding, though those symptoms are not specific.\n\nThe number only means something next to the reference range printed on your report, and next to why the test was ordered. I would not start high-dose iron from a chat. Food sources are lentils, beans, meat, and fortified grains, with fruit alongside.\n\nBring the report. Ask what range they use and whether a repeat test is needed. This is not a diagnosis."
if(A.dE(g,A.a(["tsh","thyroid"],k)))return"TSH is a signal from the pituitary to the thyroid. A high or low result can mean the thyroid is under-active or over-active, but the cutoff is the range on your lab sheet, read with symptoms and sometimes with free T4.\n\nThis reply cannot tell you that you have a thyroid condition from a question. If thyroid is already part of your context, keep the doses and follow-up with the clinician who prescribed them."
if(A.dE(g,A.a(["estradiol","estrogen test","e2"],k)))return"Estradiol swings across a cycle. A single number without the cycle day is easy to misread. In a textbook cycle, estrogen is often described as higher in the late follicular days and lower before bleeding. That sketch is not your lab.\n\nYour result has to be read against the day it was drawn and the range for that timing. This can explain the shape. It cannot replace the lab or the person who ordered it."
if(A.dE(g,A.a(["progesterone"],k)))return'Progesterone stays low until after ovulation, then rises for about a week. A blood test is sometimes used to ask whether ovulation happened, and the timing of the draw matters more than a single "good" number.\n\nA calendar cannot prove that rise. If you are timing bloodwork, ask the clinician which day they want. This reply will not call a result normal or abnormal from here.'
